---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/klondike/', label: 'Play Klondike Solitaire' }}
>
  <p>
    When you start a game of Klondike, one setting changes everything: how many cards turn over
    from the stock at a time. <strong>Draw 1</strong> turns a single card; <strong>Draw 3</strong>
    turns three. It sounds like a small difference, but it splits Klondike into two genuinely
    different games — one relaxed and winnable, one tense and demanding. This guide explains how
    they differ, what changes in scoring, and which one you should pick.
  </p>

  <h2>How the two modes differ mechanically</h2>
  <p>
    In <strong>Draw 1</strong>, every time you tap the stock, exactly one card flips onto the
    waste pile. That means <strong>every card in the stock is reachable on each pass</strong>.
    Nothing is hidden behind another card, so you can see your whole deck over the course of a
    single cycle and plan around it. This is why Draw 1 is so much more forgiving: if a card you
    need is buried, you simply keep drawing until it surfaces, and a strong player wins roughly
    <strong>35–45% or more</strong> of deals.
  </p>
  <p>
    In <a href="/klondike-draw-3/"><strong>Draw 3</strong></a>, three cards flip at once and only
    the top of that group is playable. That means <strong>only every third card is initially
    available</strong> — the two cards beneath each turned card are out of reach until you cycle
    back around. The essential Draw 3 skill is <strong>counting the stock</strong>: because
    playing a single card from the waste shifts which cards land on top during the next cycle, a
    smart player will sometimes skip a playable card now to bring a more useful one to the surface
    on the next pass. This planning requirement is what gives Draw 3 its classic, tougher feel and
    its much lower win rate of around <strong>10–15%</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The key differences at a glance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stock access</strong> — Draw 1 reaches every card each pass; Draw 3 reaches only
      every third card until you recycle.</li>
    <li><strong>Win rate</strong> — Draw 1 is roughly 35–45%+ for a strong player; Draw 3 is
      around 10–15%.</li>
    <li><strong>Skill demand</strong> — Draw 1 rewards solid fundamentals; Draw 3 adds stock
      counting and pass planning on top.</li>
    <li><strong>Feel</strong> — Draw 1 is relaxed and steady; Draw 3 is the traditional,
      Vegas-style challenge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What changes in scoring</h2>
  <p>
    The points you earn for moves are identical in both modes: 10 points for a card sent to a
    foundation, 5 points for waste-to-tableau moves and for flipping a face-down tableau card, and
    a 15-point penalty for pulling a card back off a foundation. The one scoring difference is the
    <strong>cost of recycling the waste</strong>. In Draw 1, recycling the waste pile costs
    <strong>100 points</strong> every time. In Draw 3, the first three passes are free and each
    pass <strong>after the third costs 20 points</strong>. The design nudges Draw 3 players to be
    efficient with their stock cycles rather than spinning through the deck endlessly.
  </p>

  <h2>Which should you play?</h2>
  <p>
    Pick <strong>Draw 1</strong> if you want to relax, you are learning the game, or you simply
    like winning more often. With every card reachable each pass, you can focus on good habits —
    flipping face-down cards, holding back low cards, saving empty columns for the right King —
    without the extra burden of stock counting. It is the friendliest way to enjoy Klondike and
    the best place to build your fundamentals. If you are new, start here and
    <a href="/klondike/">play a hand</a> with hints turned on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pick <strong>Draw 3</strong> when you want a real challenge and the traditional, Vegas-style
    game. The lower win rate is the point: every solved deal feels earned, and the extra planning
    layer keeps the game interesting long after Draw 1 starts to feel routine. If you already win
    comfortably in Draw 1, Draw 3 is the natural next step.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not have to commit to either one. You can <strong>switch modes anytime in Settings</strong>,
    and the change applies to your next deal. Both modes share everything else that makes
    CardHearth pleasant to play: the same <strong>numbered, shareable deals</strong> — so you can
    replay the exact same hand or send it to a friend — and the same <strong>unlimited undo</strong>,
    so you can explore a line and take it back if it dead-ends. That makes it easy to try the same
    deal in both modes and feel the difference for yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Ready to decide with your hands? <a href="/klondike/">Play Klondike Solitaire</a> in Draw 1,
    or take on the <a href="/klondike-draw-3/">Draw 3 table</a> when you want a fight. Whichever
    you choose, our <a href="/guides/how-to-win-klondike-solitaire/">strategy guide</a> will lift
    your win rate, and the <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> covers the rest of the CardHearth
    library.
  </p>
</Article>
